    ($SATQ$b7d51f84$SATQ$, $SATQ$dcfca64d-f71d-4be1-8e85-40b6ef326b64$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$42fe69a5-2772-41cd-bc33-fdc847d4ec58$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In West Africa, jalis have traditionally been keepers of information about family histories and records of important events. They have often served as teachers and advisers, too. New technologies may have changed some aspects of the role today, but jalis continue to be valued for knowing and protecting their peoples&rsquo; stories.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5513d6f1-6526-4111-8593-31fcfb60b96a","content":"<p>Even though there have been some changes in their role, jalis continue to preserve their communities&rsquo; histories.</p>"},{"id":"3998ae5d-1d6c-479f-b4b8-146f49557184","content":"<p>Although jalis have many roles, many of them like teaching best.</p>"},{"id":"9ffac124-a673-46d6-9a42-a92b3bd9d93e","content":"<p>Jalis have been entertaining the people within their communities for centuries.</p>"},{"id":"d0dee810-e86c-467b-8de8-ca8ea40af5d6","content":"<p>Technology can now do some of the things jalis used to be responsible for.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5513d6f1-6526-4111-8593-31fcfb60b96a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it best states the main idea of the text. According to the text, jalis&rsquo; traditional role has been to maintain information about families&rsquo; histories and significant events. The text goes on to say that although technological changes have altered jalis&rsquo; role somewhat, jalis are still valued for preserving the histories of their communities.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text says nothing about jalis&rsquo; views of the various tasks they perform. There is no information to support the idea that many jalis prefer teaching to other tasks.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe jalis as being sources of entertainment. Rather, jalis are presented as valued sources of knowledge. Additionally, the text gives no indication of how long jalis have been serving their communities. Choice D is incorrect because the main focus of the text is on jalis&rsquo; role and their continued value despite the effects of technology, not on what technology can now do. Although the text indicates that jalis&rsquo; role has changed as a result of technological changes, the text doesn&rsquo;t present any specific information about technology performing tasks that jalis once performed.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-003-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$08791777-0fd9-42ac-bdad-41ce4353e1b9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b7d51f84","external_id":"dcfca64d-f71d-4be1-8e85-40b6ef326b64","vaultid":"42fe69a5-2772-41cd-bc33-fdc847d4ec58","uId":"423665f3-c8e6-42ae-b3a6-59aba12fde73","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In West Africa, jalis have traditionally been keepers of information about family histories and records of important events. They have often served as teachers and advisers, too. New technologies may have changed some aspects of the role today, but jalis continue to be valued for knowing and protecting their peoples&rsquo; stories.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5513d6f1-6526-4111-8593-31fcfb60b96a","content":"<p>Even though there have been some changes in their role, jalis continue to preserve their communities&rsquo; histories.</p>"},{"id":"3998ae5d-1d6c-479f-b4b8-146f49557184","content":"<p>Although jalis have many roles, many of them like teaching best.</p>"},{"id":"9ffac124-a673-46d6-9a42-a92b3bd9d93e","content":"<p>Jalis have been entertaining the people within their communities for centuries.</p>"},{"id":"d0dee810-e86c-467b-8de8-ca8ea40af5d6","content":"<p>Technology can now do some of the things jalis used to be responsible for.</p>"}],"keys":["5513d6f1-6526-4111-8593-31fcfb60b96a"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it best states the main idea of the text. According to the text, jalis&rsquo; traditional role has been to maintain information about families&rsquo; histories and significant events. The text goes on to say that although technological changes have altered jalis&rsquo; role somewhat, jalis are still valued for preserving the histories of their communities.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text says nothing about jalis&rsquo; views of the various tasks they perform. There is no information to support the idea that many jalis prefer teaching to other tasks.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe jalis as being sources of entertainment. Rather, jalis are presented as valued sources of knowledge. Additionally, the text gives no indication of how long jalis have been serving their communities. Choice D is incorrect because the main focus of the text is on jalis&rsquo; role and their continued value despite the effects of technology, not on what technology can now do. Although the text indicates that jalis&rsquo; role has changed as a result of technological changes, the text doesn&rsquo;t present any specific information about technology performing tasks that jalis once performed.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-003-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"08791777-0fd9-42ac-bdad-41ce4353e1b9","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959312,"updateDate":1691007959312}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959312, 1691007959312),
    ($SATQ$b7f79059$SATQ$, $SATQ$c9b72f46-37cb-4778-ad91-d73d3d263723$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ca167810-2c70-4dad-94ec-d92996f0ddfe$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Ezra Pound&rsquo;s 1909 poem &ldquo;Hymn III,&rdquo; based on the work of Marcantonio Flaminio.</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em;">As a fragile and lovely flower unfolds its gleaming<br>&ensp;foliage on the breast of the fostering earth, if<br>&ensp;the dew and the rain draw it forth;<br>So doth my tender mind flourish, if it be fed with the<br>&ensp;sweet dew of the fostering spirit,<br>Lacking this, it beginneth straightway to languish,<br>&ensp;even as a floweret born upon dry earth, if the<br>&ensp;dew and the rain tend it not.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the text, in what way is the human mind like a flower?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a5115760-9fac-465e-bfed-5e7394f066e9","content":"<p>It becomes increasingly vigorous with the passage of time.</p>"},{"id":"5e681e4e-e96a-4b9e-9e29-c5de0e4730f4","content":"<p>It draws strength from changes in the weather.</p>"},{"id":"dce58823-f71e-4437-82c6-16ebfc1a4760","content":"<p>&nbsp;It requires proper nourishment in order to thrive.</p>"},{"id":"020a1180-64ef-4080-a527-e3ddb26aa97c","content":"<p>It perseveres despite challenging circumstances.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dce58823-f71e-4437-82c6-16ebfc1a4760"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a description of how the human mind is like a flower that is directly supported by the text. The text compares the needs of a &ldquo;fragile and lovely flower&rdquo; to those of the speaker&rsquo;s &ldquo;tender mind&rdquo;: both need to be fed if they&rsquo;re going to survive. Without such feeding, they&rsquo;ll &ldquo;beginneth straightway to languish,&rdquo; or weaken. Thus, the text suggests that the human mind is like a flower in that they both need proper nourishment in order to thrive. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t address the passage of time or describe either the human mind or a flower as becoming increasingly vigorous.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that human minds or flowers draw strength from changes in weather. The references to rain in the text pertain to a flower&rsquo;s need for water rather than the general effects of changing weather.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because&nbsp;the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the human mind or a flower will persist regardless of challenging circumstances. In fact, the text indicates that they&rsquo;ll both languish right away if not given what they need. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-021-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$471b2447-297f-4558-9ac0-e4124594d908$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b7f79059","external_id":"c9b72f46-37cb-4778-ad91-d73d3d263723","vaultid":"ca167810-2c70-4dad-94ec-d92996f0ddfe","uId":"a613dde0-652e-4e97-b3eb-9f22774dbe30","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Ezra Pound&rsquo;s 1909 poem &ldquo;Hymn III,&rdquo; based on the work of Marcantonio Flaminio.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em;\">As a fragile and lovely flower unfolds its gleaming<br>&ensp;foliage on the breast of the fostering earth, if<br>&ensp;the dew and the rain draw it forth;<br>So doth my tender mind flourish, if it be fed with the<br>&ensp;sweet dew of the fostering spirit,<br>Lacking this, it beginneth straightway to languish,<br>&ensp;even as a floweret born upon dry earth, if the<br>&ensp;dew and the rain tend it not.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the text, in what way is the human mind like a flower?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a5115760-9fac-465e-bfed-5e7394f066e9","content":"<p>It becomes increasingly vigorous with the passage of time.</p>"},{"id":"5e681e4e-e96a-4b9e-9e29-c5de0e4730f4","content":"<p>It draws strength from changes in the weather.</p>"},{"id":"dce58823-f71e-4437-82c6-16ebfc1a4760","content":"<p>&nbsp;It requires proper nourishment in order to thrive.</p>"},{"id":"020a1180-64ef-4080-a527-e3ddb26aa97c","content":"<p>It perseveres despite challenging circumstances.</p>"}],"keys":["dce58823-f71e-4437-82c6-16ebfc1a4760"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a description of how the human mind is like a flower that is directly supported by the text. The text compares the needs of a &ldquo;fragile and lovely flower&rdquo; to those of the speaker&rsquo;s &ldquo;tender mind&rdquo;: both need to be fed if they&rsquo;re going to survive. Without such feeding, they&rsquo;ll &ldquo;beginneth straightway to languish,&rdquo; or weaken. Thus, the text suggests that the human mind is like a flower in that they both need proper nourishment in order to thrive. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t address the passage of time or describe either the human mind or a flower as becoming increasingly vigorous.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that human minds or flowers draw strength from changes in weather. The references to rain in the text pertain to a flower&rsquo;s need for water rather than the general effects of changing weather.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because&nbsp;the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the human mind or a flower will persist regardless of challenging circumstances. In fact, the text indicates that they&rsquo;ll both languish right away if not given what they need. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-021-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"471b2447-297f-4558-9ac0-e4124594d908","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$b8199d5a$SATQ$, $SATQ$e19865c4-0bd8-47e7-bbbd-847b84b95b25$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Total Areas and 2022 Populations of Smallest Arabian Peninsula Countries</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Country</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Total area (square miles)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Population</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Kuwait</th><td style="text-align: center;">6,880</td><td style="text-align: center;">4,268,873</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Bahrain</th><td style="text-align: center;">304</td><td style="text-align: center;">1,472,233</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Qatar</th><td style="text-align: center;">4,471</td><td style="text-align: center;">2,695,122</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>In terms of area and population, the three smallest Arabian Peninsula countries are Bahrain, Qatar, and Kuwait.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the table, what is the total area of Bahrain?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"919dc131-d6d8-4053-aab7-ce2c421147af","content":"<p>4,268,873 square miles</p>"},{"id":"1ba82a99-aa43-4bd7-8e40-d8de8e9ad318","content":"<p>4,471 square miles</p>"},{"id":"190e7f2b-dd8a-4c26-9b14-1957b95ef31c","content":"<p>304 square miles</p>"},{"id":"b48208a1-6c80-4e6e-88af-34b0f849ea7f","content":"<p>6,880 square miles</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["190e7f2b-dd8a-4c26-9b14-1957b95ef31c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it states the total area of Bahrain that is indicated in the table. The table presents the total area (in square miles) and population for Bahrain, Qatar, and Kuwait, and it indicates that the total area of Bahrain is 304 square miles.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table indicates that 4,268,873 is the population of Kuwait, not the total area of Bahrain. Choice B is incorrect because the table indicates that 4,471 square miles is the total area of Qatar, not of Bahrain. Choice D is incorrect because the table indicates that 6,880 square miles is the total area of Kuwait, not of Bahrain.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b8199d5a","external_id":"e19865c4-0bd8-47e7-bbbd-847b84b95b25","vaultid":null,"uId":"45b730dd-18cb-4736-ae92-f85e91b44104","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Total Areas and 2022 Populations of Smallest Arabian Peninsula Countries</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Country</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Total area (square miles)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Population</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Kuwait</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">6,880</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">4,268,873</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Bahrain</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">304</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1,472,233</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Qatar</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">4,471</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2,695,122</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>In terms of area and population, the three smallest Arabian Peninsula countries are Bahrain, Qatar, and Kuwait.</p>","stem":"<p>According to the table, what is the total area of Bahrain?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"919dc131-d6d8-4053-aab7-ce2c421147af","content":"<p>4,268,873 square miles</p>"},{"id":"1ba82a99-aa43-4bd7-8e40-d8de8e9ad318","content":"<p>4,471 square miles</p>"},{"id":"190e7f2b-dd8a-4c26-9b14-1957b95ef31c","content":"<p>304 square miles</p>"},{"id":"b48208a1-6c80-4e6e-88af-34b0f849ea7f","content":"<p>6,880 square miles</p>"}],"keys":["190e7f2b-dd8a-4c26-9b14-1957b95ef31c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it states the total area of Bahrain that is indicated in the table. The table presents the total area (in square miles) and population for Bahrain, Qatar, and Kuwait, and it indicates that the total area of Bahrain is 304 square miles.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table indicates that 4,268,873 is the population of Kuwait, not the total area of Bahrain. Choice B is incorrect because the table indicates that 4,471 square miles is the total area of Qatar, not of Bahrain. Choice D is incorrect because the table indicates that 6,880 square miles is the total area of Kuwait, not of Bahrain.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$baef99a5$SATQ$, $SATQ$03508ded-7e44-467a-b16b-93608113408c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3069e93d-4da5-443f-8e39-1a73230a1ed9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde&rsquo;s 1891 novel <em>The Picture of Dorian Gray</em>. Dorian Gray is taking his first look at a portrait that Hallward has painted of him.</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em">Dorian passed listlessly in front of his picture and turned towards it. When he saw it he drew back, and his cheeks flushed for a moment with pleasure. A look of joy came into his eyes, as if he had recognized himself for the first time. He stood there motionless and in wonder, dimly conscious that Hallward was speaking to him, but not catching the meaning of his words. The sense of his own beauty came on him like a revelation. He had never felt it before. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the text, what is true about Dorian?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"192250c8-6e7b-4592-a49c-5c10e7766687","content":"<p>He wants to know Hallward&rsquo;s opinion of the portrait.</p>"},{"id":"a2ec49d7-ef12-4453-b1a5-9c832cf50693","content":"<p>He is delighted by what he sees in the portrait.</p>"},{"id":"9a0bedbd-8930-463c-9501-135f501ca95d","content":"<p>He prefers portraits to other types of paintings.</p>"},{"id":"74035146-0534-4e08-b3ee-85547f3fc72f","content":"<p>He is uncertain of Hallward&rsquo;s talent as an artist.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a2ec49d7-ef12-4453-b1a5-9c832cf50693"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a statement about Dorian that is directly supported by the text. The narrator of the text says that when Dorian sees his portrait, &ldquo;his cheeks flushed for a moment with pleasure&rdquo; and &ldquo;a look of joy came into his eyes.&rdquo; The narrator goes on to say that Dorian looked at the portrait &ldquo;in wonder&rdquo; and presents him as being so entranced by the portrait that he doesn&rsquo;t notice what Hallward is saying to him. All these details support the description of Dorian as being delighted by what he sees in the portrait. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Dorian isn&rsquo;t depicted as interested in Hallward&rsquo;s opinion of the portrait but rather as so enraptured by the painting that he&rsquo;s hardly even aware of Hallward.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the portrait of Dorian is the only painting that is mentioned in the text, so there&rsquo;s no evidence that Dorian prefers portraits to other types of paintings. Although Dorian is depicted as delighted with this particular portrait, there&rsquo;s no way of knowing from the text whether he likes portraits better than other kinds of paintings. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Dorian is uncertain about Hallward&rsquo;s talent. Instead, the text is focused on Dorian&rsquo;s delight with the portrait.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-011-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e1e6db55-0f78-41eb-a8d0-3e1c7cc008c1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"baef99a5","external_id":"03508ded-7e44-467a-b16b-93608113408c","vaultid":"3069e93d-4da5-443f-8e39-1a73230a1ed9","uId":"3f577c83-3685-417c-b303-06c3994e6fca","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde&rsquo;s 1891 novel <em>The Picture of Dorian Gray</em>. Dorian Gray is taking his first look at a portrait that Hallward has painted of him.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em\">Dorian passed listlessly in front of his picture and turned towards it. When he saw it he drew back, and his cheeks flushed for a moment with pleasure. A look of joy came into his eyes, as if he had recognized himself for the first time. He stood there motionless and in wonder, dimly conscious that Hallward was speaking to him, but not catching the meaning of his words. The sense of his own beauty came on him like a revelation. He had never felt it before. </p>","stem":"<p>According to the text, what is true about Dorian?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"192250c8-6e7b-4592-a49c-5c10e7766687","content":"<p>He wants to know Hallward&rsquo;s opinion of the portrait.</p>"},{"id":"a2ec49d7-ef12-4453-b1a5-9c832cf50693","content":"<p>He is delighted by what he sees in the portrait.</p>"},{"id":"9a0bedbd-8930-463c-9501-135f501ca95d","content":"<p>He prefers portraits to other types of paintings.</p>"},{"id":"74035146-0534-4e08-b3ee-85547f3fc72f","content":"<p>He is uncertain of Hallward&rsquo;s talent as an artist.</p>"}],"keys":["a2ec49d7-ef12-4453-b1a5-9c832cf50693"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a statement about Dorian that is directly supported by the text. The narrator of the text says that when Dorian sees his portrait, &ldquo;his cheeks flushed for a moment with pleasure&rdquo; and &ldquo;a look of joy came into his eyes.&rdquo; The narrator goes on to say that Dorian looked at the portrait &ldquo;in wonder&rdquo; and presents him as being so entranced by the portrait that he doesn&rsquo;t notice what Hallward is saying to him. All these details support the description of Dorian as being delighted by what he sees in the portrait. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Dorian isn&rsquo;t depicted as interested in Hallward&rsquo;s opinion of the portrait but rather as so enraptured by the painting that he&rsquo;s hardly even aware of Hallward.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the portrait of Dorian is the only painting that is mentioned in the text, so there&rsquo;s no evidence that Dorian prefers portraits to other types of paintings. Although Dorian is depicted as delighted with this particular portrait, there&rsquo;s no way of knowing from the text whether he likes portraits better than other kinds of paintings. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Dorian is uncertain about Hallward&rsquo;s talent. Instead, the text is focused on Dorian&rsquo;s delight with the portrait.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-011-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e1e6db55-0f78-41eb-a8d0-3e1c7cc008c1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959314,"updateDate":1691007959314}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959314, 1691007959314),
    ($SATQ$bbfa2bb6$SATQ$, $SATQ$9cb7ff11-cb19-42f9-b8d2-f5191ec6866c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>Hevea brasiliensis</em>, a tree in the Amazon rainforest, is the world&rsquo;s main source of natural rubber. The tree produces a milky substance called latex that is used to make rubber. The bark of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is helpful for the process of making rubber because it has a unique structure that makes it easy to collect latex. A network of tubes in the tree&rsquo;s inner bark helps the latex to flow out easily when people make small cuts into the bark.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>What feature of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> does the text say is helpful for the process of making rubber?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"380b15f9-2443-43fc-aee7-da38e8e51255","content":"<p>Its latex produces rubber of an especially high quality.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"00b9566b-f21e-40f2-8843-c76ce7db0ce8","content":"<p>Its bark has a unique structure that makes it easy to collect latex.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c897cd5c-ca26-4be6-90aa-93da5e92221b","content":"<p>It is able to grow in a wide variety of climates around the world.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f927cd61-81cd-4a45-a3f8-0e1ba6e551fc","content":"<p>It is one of only two trees in the Amazon that produce latex.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["00b9566b-f21e-40f2-8843-c76ce7db0ce8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately states what feature of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is helpful for the process of making rubber. According to the text, this tree species produces latex, which is used to make rubber, and its inner bark contains a "network of tubes" that, when cut, enables the latex to flow out. The text explicitly states that this feature of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is "helpful for the process of making rubber."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention the quality of the rubber produced from the latex of <em>Hevea brasiliensis </em>or compare its quality to that of rubber produced from other sources. Choice C is incorrect because the text never discusses the climates in which <em>Hevea brasiliensis </em>grows. Moreover, the text mentions only one region where this tree is found: the Amazon rainforest. Choice D is incorrect. Because the text states that <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is the world&rsquo;s "main source of natural rubber," it can be inferred that there is at least one other source. However, the text doesn&rsquo;t specify whether that other source is also a tree species and, if so, whether that species grows in the Amazon rainforest.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bbfa2bb6","external_id":"9cb7ff11-cb19-42f9-b8d2-f5191ec6866c","vaultid":null,"uId":"cc470f28-f525-4904-9a65-2baea6e0ee55","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>Hevea brasiliensis</em>, a tree in the Amazon rainforest, is the world&rsquo;s main source of natural rubber. The tree produces a milky substance called latex that is used to make rubber. The bark of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is helpful for the process of making rubber because it has a unique structure that makes it easy to collect latex. A network of tubes in the tree&rsquo;s inner bark helps the latex to flow out easily when people make small cuts into the bark.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>What feature of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> does the text say is helpful for the process of making rubber?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"380b15f9-2443-43fc-aee7-da38e8e51255","content":"<p>Its latex produces rubber of an especially high quality.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"00b9566b-f21e-40f2-8843-c76ce7db0ce8","content":"<p>Its bark has a unique structure that makes it easy to collect latex.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c897cd5c-ca26-4be6-90aa-93da5e92221b","content":"<p>It is able to grow in a wide variety of climates around the world.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f927cd61-81cd-4a45-a3f8-0e1ba6e551fc","content":"<p>It is one of only two trees in the Amazon that produce latex.</p>"}],"keys":["00b9566b-f21e-40f2-8843-c76ce7db0ce8"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately states what feature of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is helpful for the process of making rubber. According to the text, this tree species produces latex, which is used to make rubber, and its inner bark contains a \"network of tubes\" that, when cut, enables the latex to flow out. The text explicitly states that this feature of <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is \"helpful for the process of making rubber.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention the quality of the rubber produced from the latex of <em>Hevea brasiliensis </em>or compare its quality to that of rubber produced from other sources. Choice C is incorrect because the text never discusses the climates in which <em>Hevea brasiliensis </em>grows. Moreover, the text mentions only one region where this tree is found: the Amazon rainforest. Choice D is incorrect. Because the text states that <em>Hevea brasiliensis</em> is the world&rsquo;s \"main source of natural rubber,\" it can be inferred that there is at least one other source. However, the text doesn&rsquo;t specify whether that other source is also a tree species and, if so, whether that species grows in the Amazon rainforest.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669211,"updateDate":1743430669211}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669211, 1743430669211),
    ($SATQ$bc1b8a42$SATQ$, $SATQ$738bb8cf-0cb8-43fe-8790-a8d9e93bfa3a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Percentage of Bus Shelters with Shade in a County by Areas’ Highest Average Summer Surface Temperature</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Highest average surface temperature (Fahrenheit)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">90.2&deg;</th><td style="text-align: center;">15%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">97.7&deg;</th><td style="text-align: center;">22%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">102.7&deg;</th><td style="text-align: center;">24%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">111.2&deg;</th><td style="text-align: center;">28%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">125.6&deg;</th><td style="text-align: center;">29%</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>A student is researching a bus system in a large county where surface temperatures vary by area and are hot in the summer. The student claims that all areas of the county should have more bus stops with shaded shelter, noting that the highest percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter for any area is only <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the student&rsquo;s claim?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ca1b45c5-23b0-458b-88d6-9c5a9d9f727c","content":"<p>50%.</p>"},{"id":"36957aa9-7668-4340-82b4-987b3c0d5c8a","content":"<p>15%.</p>"},{"id":"1a179b61-23f3-48ce-9d97-817f51b5d4ad","content":"<p>90%.</p>"},{"id":"6eeb7ce1-1d6d-4b65-b26a-7b4ddc862fa8","content":"<p>29%.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6eeb7ce1-1d6d-4b65-b26a-7b4ddc862fa8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it effectively uses data from the table to complete the student&rsquo;s claim about the highest percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter in the areas represented. The table shows the highest average surface temperature of five areas and the percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter in each area, and 29% is the highest percentage listed.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the list of percentages of bus stops with shaded shelter does not include 50%; the highest percentage in the table is 29%. Choice B is incorrect because 15% is the lowest value in the listed percentages of bus stops with shaded shelter, not the highest value. Choice C is incorrect because the list of percentages of bus stops with shaded shelter does not include 90%; the highest percentage in the table is 29%.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bc1b8a42","external_id":"738bb8cf-0cb8-43fe-8790-a8d9e93bfa3a","vaultid":null,"uId":"22038d95-c83f-4c0f-ae10-7e1550fd7436","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Percentage of Bus Shelters with Shade in a County by Areas’ Highest Average Summer Surface Temperature</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Highest average surface temperature (Fahrenheit)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">90.2&deg;</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">15%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">97.7&deg;</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">22%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">102.7&deg;</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">24%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">111.2&deg;</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">28%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">125.6&deg;</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">29%</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>A student is researching a bus system in a large county where surface temperatures vary by area and are hot in the summer. The student claims that all areas of the county should have more bus stops with shaded shelter, noting that the highest percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter for any area is only <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the student&rsquo;s claim?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ca1b45c5-23b0-458b-88d6-9c5a9d9f727c","content":"<p>50%.</p>"},{"id":"36957aa9-7668-4340-82b4-987b3c0d5c8a","content":"<p>15%.</p>"},{"id":"1a179b61-23f3-48ce-9d97-817f51b5d4ad","content":"<p>90%.</p>"},{"id":"6eeb7ce1-1d6d-4b65-b26a-7b4ddc862fa8","content":"<p>29%.</p>"}],"keys":["6eeb7ce1-1d6d-4b65-b26a-7b4ddc862fa8"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it effectively uses data from the table to complete the student&rsquo;s claim about the highest percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter in the areas represented. The table shows the highest average surface temperature of five areas and the percentage of bus stops with shaded shelter in each area, and 29% is the highest percentage listed.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the list of percentages of bus stops with shaded shelter does not include 50%; the highest percentage in the table is 29%. Choice B is incorrect because 15% is the lowest value in the listed percentages of bus stops with shaded shelter, not the highest value. Choice C is incorrect because the list of percentages of bus stops with shaded shelter does not include 90%; the highest percentage in the table is 29%.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$bc7b1a04$SATQ$, $SATQ$de7f8b22-30e6-43b1-ad24-4b6f6f7e025f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Three Studies' Estimated Average Velocity of LMC</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Researchers</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Study year</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Estimated average velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Murai and Fujimoto</th><td style="text-align: center;">1980</td><td style="text-align: center;">344 km/s</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Kallivayalil and colleagues</th><td style="text-align: center;">2006</td><td style="text-align: center;">378 km/s</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Gardiner and colleagues</th><td style="text-align: center;">1994</td><td style="text-align: center;">297 km/s</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>In 2006, Nitya Kallivayalil and colleagues calculated the most accurate estimate yet of the average velocity (in kilometers per second) of the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) galaxy. Before the 2006 study, estimates of the average velocity were low enough for the LMC to maintain an orbit around the Milky Way galaxy, but according to an analysis by Gurtina Besla and colleagues, the estimated velocity from the 2006 study is too high for the LMC to maintain such an orbit. Therefore, if Besla and colleagues are correct, the maximum average velocity for the LMC that would allow it to maintain orbit around the Milky Way is likely <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"83a03161-3ba0-44c9-9fd4-34a707f29e64","content":"<p>above 344 km/s but below 378 km/s.</p>"},{"id":"64c87eff-9589-4aed-9d94-ed4e10a2f961","content":"<p>above 297 km/s but below 344 km/s.</p>"},{"id":"29c61bee-f8de-4936-afd4-79a653876e4d","content":"<p>above 378 km/s.</p>"},{"id":"be0af845-eea1-40ab-84a3-3ae555cf1c95","content":"<p>below 297 km/s.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["83a03161-3ba0-44c9-9fd4-34a707f29e64"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it uses data from the table to give the range of velocities for the LMC from the 1980 value (344 km/s) to the 2006 value (378 km/s), thereby effectively completing the text. The text indicates that before 2006, all the estimated velocities of the LMC were within the range necessary to maintain orbit around the Milky Way galaxy. It then indicates that, according to Besla and colleagues, the 2006 estimate of 378 km/s is too high to maintain that orbit. This strongly implies that if the 1980 value (344 km/s) is below the orbital threshold, and if Besla and colleagues are correct that the 2006 value (378 km/s) is above that threshold, the maximum orbital velocity for the LMC must be somewhere in the range from above 344 km/s to below 378 km/s.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The text indicates that the 2006 velocity estimate for the LMC (378 km/s) was the first estimate that exceeded the velocity needed to maintain orbit around the Milky Way. Thus, the 1980 estimate of 344 km/s and the 1994 estimate of 297 km/s must both be below the maximum possible orbital velocity for the LMC. Choice C is incorrect. The text states that Besla and colleagues&rsquo; analysis found that the velocity from the 2006 study (378 km/s) was too high for the LMC to maintain orbit around the Milky Way. Therefore, if a velocity of 378 km/s is too high, an even higher velocity will also be too high. Choice D is incorrect. The text indicates that the 2006 velocity estimate for the LMC (378 km/s) was the first to exceed the velocity range required to maintain orbit around the Milky Way. Thus, the 1994 estimate of 297 km/s must be below the maximum possible orbital velocity for the LMC.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bc7b1a04","external_id":"de7f8b22-30e6-43b1-ad24-4b6f6f7e025f","vaultid":null,"uId":"12813304-43b1-49bf-a5f9-294875433005","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Three Studies' Estimated Average Velocity of LMC</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Researchers</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Study year</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Estimated average velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Murai and Fujimoto</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1980</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">344 km/s</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Kallivayalil and colleagues</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2006</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">378 km/s</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Gardiner and colleagues</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1994</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">297 km/s</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>In 2006, Nitya Kallivayalil and colleagues calculated the most accurate estimate yet of the average velocity (in kilometers per second) of the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) galaxy. Before the 2006 study, estimates of the average velocity were low enough for the LMC to maintain an orbit around the Milky Way galaxy, but according to an analysis by Gurtina Besla and colleagues, the estimated velocity from the 2006 study is too high for the LMC to maintain such an orbit. Therefore, if Besla and colleagues are correct, the maximum average velocity for the LMC that would allow it to maintain orbit around the Milky Way is likely <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"83a03161-3ba0-44c9-9fd4-34a707f29e64","content":"<p>above 344 km/s but below 378 km/s.</p>"},{"id":"64c87eff-9589-4aed-9d94-ed4e10a2f961","content":"<p>above 297 km/s but below 344 km/s.</p>"},{"id":"29c61bee-f8de-4936-afd4-79a653876e4d","content":"<p>above 378 km/s.</p>"},{"id":"be0af845-eea1-40ab-84a3-3ae555cf1c95","content":"<p>below 297 km/s.</p>"}],"keys":["83a03161-3ba0-44c9-9fd4-34a707f29e64"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it uses data from the table to give the range of velocities for the LMC from the 1980 value (344 km/s) to the 2006 value (378 km/s), thereby effectively completing the text. The text indicates that before 2006, all the estimated velocities of the LMC were within the range necessary to maintain orbit around the Milky Way galaxy. It then indicates that, according to Besla and colleagues, the 2006 estimate of 378 km/s is too high to maintain that orbit. This strongly implies that if the 1980 value (344 km/s) is below the orbital threshold, and if Besla and colleagues are correct that the 2006 value (378 km/s) is above that threshold, the maximum orbital velocity for the LMC must be somewhere in the range from above 344 km/s to below 378 km/s.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The text indicates that the 2006 velocity estimate for the LMC (378 km/s) was the first estimate that exceeded the velocity needed to maintain orbit around the Milky Way. Thus, the 1980 estimate of 344 km/s and the 1994 estimate of 297 km/s must both be below the maximum possible orbital velocity for the LMC. Choice C is incorrect. The text states that Besla and colleagues&rsquo; analysis found that the velocity from the 2006 study (378 km/s) was too high for the LMC to maintain orbit around the Milky Way. Therefore, if a velocity of 378 km/s is too high, an even higher velocity will also be too high. Choice D is incorrect. The text indicates that the 2006 velocity estimate for the LMC (378 km/s) was the first to exceed the velocity range required to maintain orbit around the Milky Way. Thus, the 1994 estimate of 297 km/s must be below the maximum possible orbital velocity for the LMC.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323046,"updateDate":1730147323046}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323046, 1730147323046),
    ($SATQ$bcbcc43f$SATQ$, $SATQ$7321ec71-5cd9-4eb9-93f9-7e268cefccba$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f8d66f9c-e54a-4963-b234-eeacdab21b81$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The ancient Sumerian civilization formed around 4000 BCE between two large rivers in an area that is now Iraq and Syria. The extremely hot and sunny weather in that area helped crops grow very quickly, but it also made it hard to keep the crops from drying up and dying. So, the Sumerians used water from the rivers in their farming. That method worked so well that they often could harvest even more crops than they needed in a season. As a result, the Sumerians <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6acb4530-7faa-4ea9-9d00-063b8834471a","content":"<p>harvested crops only on the hottest days of each season.</p>"},{"id":"9589d3f7-3141-42a1-8cb5-27549374656d","content":"<p>found ways to shield their crops from the sun.</p>"},{"id":"2d99e3c9-2f55-4585-9464-406276798e0f","content":"<p>did not begin farming until long after 4000 BCE.</p>"},{"id":"3d2b0be2-2d40-4684-8a01-b019f4ef55f2","content":"<p>were able to store extra crops for later use.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3d2b0be2-2d40-4684-8a01-b019f4ef55f2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents the conclusion that most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Sumerian civilization and crop growth. The text mentions the hot, sunny weather in the area where the Sumerians lived, which made crops grow quickly but also made it difficult to keep them alive. The Sumerians solved this problem by using river water for their farming&mdash;as a result, they often harvested more crops than were needed in a season. It follows that the Sumerians must have needed to find something to do with the surplus crops&mdash;that is, they stored the extra crops for later use. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t logically follow that a surplus in crops would lead the Sumerians to choose only certain days of the season to harvest. Nor is there any indication in the text that the Sumerians improved their farming methods with the goal of reducing the time spent farming. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the Sumerians tried to shield their crops from the sun: in fact, the text indicates that the sunny weather helped crops grow very quickly and that the Sumerians used river water to allow crops to be exposed to the sun without dying. Choice C is incorrect. Having a surplus of crops wouldn&rsquo;t have caused the Sumerians to begin farming until long after 4000 BCE: in fact, since the text indicates that the Sumerian civilization formed around 4000 BCE and farming was a part of that civilization, the statement that Sumerians only began farming long after 4000 BCE isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-030-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3af72bf5-ebb6-4d81-8146-00e69a1a3735$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bcbcc43f","external_id":"7321ec71-5cd9-4eb9-93f9-7e268cefccba","vaultid":"f8d66f9c-e54a-4963-b234-eeacdab21b81","uId":"cf3346f3-60e5-4a2b-90dc-56231951013f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The ancient Sumerian civilization formed around 4000 BCE between two large rivers in an area that is now Iraq and Syria. The extremely hot and sunny weather in that area helped crops grow very quickly, but it also made it hard to keep the crops from drying up and dying. So, the Sumerians used water from the rivers in their farming. That method worked so well that they often could harvest even more crops than they needed in a season. As a result, the Sumerians <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6acb4530-7faa-4ea9-9d00-063b8834471a","content":"<p>harvested crops only on the hottest days of each season.</p>"},{"id":"9589d3f7-3141-42a1-8cb5-27549374656d","content":"<p>found ways to shield their crops from the sun.</p>"},{"id":"2d99e3c9-2f55-4585-9464-406276798e0f","content":"<p>did not begin farming until long after 4000 BCE.</p>"},{"id":"3d2b0be2-2d40-4684-8a01-b019f4ef55f2","content":"<p>were able to store extra crops for later use.</p>"}],"keys":["3d2b0be2-2d40-4684-8a01-b019f4ef55f2"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents the conclusion that most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Sumerian civilization and crop growth. The text mentions the hot, sunny weather in the area where the Sumerians lived, which made crops grow quickly but also made it difficult to keep them alive. The Sumerians solved this problem by using river water for their farming&mdash;as a result, they often harvested more crops than were needed in a season. It follows that the Sumerians must have needed to find something to do with the surplus crops&mdash;that is, they stored the extra crops for later use. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t logically follow that a surplus in crops would lead the Sumerians to choose only certain days of the season to harvest. Nor is there any indication in the text that the Sumerians improved their farming methods with the goal of reducing the time spent farming. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the Sumerians tried to shield their crops from the sun: in fact, the text indicates that the sunny weather helped crops grow very quickly and that the Sumerians used river water to allow crops to be exposed to the sun without dying. Choice C is incorrect. Having a surplus of crops wouldn&rsquo;t have caused the Sumerians to begin farming until long after 4000 BCE: in fact, since the text indicates that the Sumerian civilization formed around 4000 BCE and farming was a part of that civilization, the statement that Sumerians only began farming long after 4000 BCE isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-030-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3af72bf5-ebb6-4d81-8146-00e69a1a3735","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959318,"updateDate":1691007959318}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959318, 1691007959318),
    ($SATQ$bcf2f169$SATQ$, $SATQ$611815f9-9f7f-45ef-b59d-26119ee9c308$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$386f156f-f1ff-4ec8-ba6d-18fe41f3761f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ana Castillo&rsquo;s 1986 novel <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> is a story told entirely through expressive letters from the narrator to her friend&mdash;letters that Castillo suggests could be read in several different orders. As they began reading it in class, some students remarked that they found the novel&rsquo;s letter format daunting and its treatment of gender relations old-fashioned. The professor, however, pointed out that the novel is written in modern-sounding language and addresses issues that still matter today, suggesting that <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8293cdf1-ee62-41b2-85a4-ec34ac592ad7","content":"<p>has more to say about gender relations than other novels from the same period.</p>"},{"id":"3c01bee7-fa80-4c88-9f24-b9c2dcffe3ed","content":"<p>is more relevant to contemporary audiences than it may seem at first.</p>"},{"id":"e270722b-7f57-47e4-acbf-cc32e0a819a5","content":"<p>is easier to read than many contemporary novels that focus on friendship.</p>"},{"id":"f9e0b557-d34f-4bab-954e-59277274fe89","content":"<p>is best understood after multiple readings in different orders.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3c01bee7-fa80-4c88-9f24-b9c2dcffe3ed"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Ana Castillo&rsquo;s 1986 novel <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em>. The text states that the novel consists entirely of letters from the narrator to her friend&mdash;a format that some students reading the novel in a class found intimidating. According to the text, those students also found the novel&rsquo;s treatment of gender to be old-fashioned. In response to the students&rsquo; concerns, their professor emphasized the novel&rsquo;s relevance: it&rsquo;s written in modern-sounding language and addresses issues that still matter. This, in turn, suggests that <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> is more relevant to contemporary audiences than it may initially seem. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the professor&rsquo;s response to the students only mentions <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em>: it doesn&rsquo;t compare the novel to others from the same period. Choice C is incorrect because nothing in the professor&rsquo;s response to the students compares <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> to contemporary novels about friendship. Choice D is incorrect because the professor&rsquo;s response to the students doesn&rsquo;t address the idea of reading the novel&rsquo;s letters multiple times and in different orders. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-039-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4e041ea4-f16b-4cae-b339-d3c653390068$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bcf2f169","external_id":"611815f9-9f7f-45ef-b59d-26119ee9c308","vaultid":"386f156f-f1ff-4ec8-ba6d-18fe41f3761f","uId":"e64d6217-2288-4196-b5f8-d2c99c5bd8c7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ana Castillo&rsquo;s 1986 novel <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> is a story told entirely through expressive letters from the narrator to her friend&mdash;letters that Castillo suggests could be read in several different orders. As they began reading it in class, some students remarked that they found the novel&rsquo;s letter format daunting and its treatment of gender relations old-fashioned. The professor, however, pointed out that the novel is written in modern-sounding language and addresses issues that still matter today, suggesting that <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8293cdf1-ee62-41b2-85a4-ec34ac592ad7","content":"<p>has more to say about gender relations than other novels from the same period.</p>"},{"id":"3c01bee7-fa80-4c88-9f24-b9c2dcffe3ed","content":"<p>is more relevant to contemporary audiences than it may seem at first.</p>"},{"id":"e270722b-7f57-47e4-acbf-cc32e0a819a5","content":"<p>is easier to read than many contemporary novels that focus on friendship.</p>"},{"id":"f9e0b557-d34f-4bab-954e-59277274fe89","content":"<p>is best understood after multiple readings in different orders.</p>"}],"keys":["3c01bee7-fa80-4c88-9f24-b9c2dcffe3ed"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Ana Castillo&rsquo;s 1986 novel <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em>. The text states that the novel consists entirely of letters from the narrator to her friend&mdash;a format that some students reading the novel in a class found intimidating. According to the text, those students also found the novel&rsquo;s treatment of gender to be old-fashioned. In response to the students&rsquo; concerns, their professor emphasized the novel&rsquo;s relevance: it&rsquo;s written in modern-sounding language and addresses issues that still matter. This, in turn, suggests that <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> is more relevant to contemporary audiences than it may initially seem. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the professor&rsquo;s response to the students only mentions <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em>: it doesn&rsquo;t compare the novel to others from the same period. Choice C is incorrect because nothing in the professor&rsquo;s response to the students compares <em>The Mixquiahuala Letters</em> to contemporary novels about friendship. Choice D is incorrect because the professor&rsquo;s response to the students doesn&rsquo;t address the idea of reading the novel&rsquo;s letters multiple times and in different orders. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-039-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4e041ea4-f16b-4cae-b339-d3c653390068","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959321,"updateDate":1691007959321}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959321, 1691007959321),
